species-lore-human-lore = Humans are the most widespread sapient species in the Galaxy, originating from Earth.
species-lore-human-feature-1 = A versatile species without extreme innate biological needs.
species-lore-human-roleplay-1 = Use a homeworld, profession, accent, or personal habits to give the character identity.
species-lore-reptilian-lore = Unathi are large scaled humanoids from a hot world, shaped by clan customs and honour.
species-lore-reptilian-feature-1 = They endure heat better than cold and prefer meat-based food.
species-lore-reptilian-communication-1 = Unathi names and speech often use hissing sounds; directness and visible gestures are not impolite by themselves.
species-lore-reptilian-communication-2 = Sinta’Unathi words can be used in moderation: “Zo” is a greeting, “Oz” a farewell, “Khar” hunting or battle, “Skhassas” death, and “Rizkh” a deity.
species-lore-reptilian-interaction-1 = Treat personal honour and agreements with respect. Blunt criticism is fine, but it is better stated directly than hinted at.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-1 = Be direct, expressive, and mindful of honour without making every character identical.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-naming = Use a Sinta’Unathi name or its meaningful Common translation, not a random string of hissing sounds.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-appearance = Muted, natural scale tones suit their appearance. A bright unusual colour should have a character-history explanation.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-gestures = Use hissing, growling, tail strikes, an open mouth, or body posture to show emotion. These complement speech rather than replace it.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-selfreliance = Independence, initiative, and a wish to solve a problem personally are appropriate. Ask for help when the situation truly calls for it.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-contact = Do not force physical contact or touch a tail without permission: it can be seen as a threat. Accusations of weakness or stupidity are especially painful to many Unathi.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-footwear = Footwraps or suitable footwear are more comfortable for their paws. Ordinary boots can be roleplayed as uncomfortable where needed.
species-lore-reptilian-roleplay-traditions = Religion, gender roles, and attitudes to authority differ between clans. Choose the character’s own stance toward tradition rather than treating it as mandatory for all.
species-lore-slime-lore = Slime people are sapient gelatinous beings whose biology differs greatly from that of humans.
species-lore-slime-feature-1 = They breathe nitrogen rather than oxygen.
species-lore-slime-feature-2 = Water, cold, and the environment require extra care.
species-lore-slime-roleplay-1 = Express emotion through colour, fluid movement, and an unusual view of body shape.
species-lore-dwarf-lore = Dwarves are short humanoids with strong artisan and community traditions.
species-lore-dwarf-feature-1 = Their short stature is a defining visual trait.
species-lore-dwarf-roleplay-1 = Practicality, craft pride, and respect for quality work make good roleplay hooks.
species-lore-diona-lore = Diona, also called Dionea, are collective plant beings formed from many nymphs into one mind. They have no universally recognised homeworld: many gestalt minds travel, study other societies, and gladly help where they are useful.
species-lore-diona-feature-1 = Their perception of personhood and time may differ from humans.
species-lore-diona-communication-1 = A gestalt may speak as “we,” pause after internal discussion, or let one nymph speak for the whole.
species-lore-diona-communication-2 = Speech depends on gestalt type: “we,” “I,” “majority,” and “minority” are deliberate options, not random pronoun changes.
species-lore-diona-interaction-1 = Younger gestalts often respect elders and regard other species with curiosity; they seek peaceful solutions first, but do not have to be helpless.
species-lore-diona-roleplay-1 = A calm, thoughtful manner, curiosity toward other cultures, and care for individual nymphs fit well; a collective nature does not erase a gestalt's own character.
species-lore-diona-roleplay-naming = Follow Diona naming conventions. Consider the gestalt’s age, origin, and who it was before working aboard the station.
species-lore-diona-roleplay-unified = “Unified Voice”: a coordinated gestalt fully aware of itself as one person. Usually speaks as “we.”
species-lore-diona-roleplay-chorus = “Collective Chorus”: nymphs coordinate well and process much information. Usually speaks as “we.”
species-lore-diona-roleplay-melody = “Inner Melody”: decisions are taken by internal vote; “majority” and “minority” fit its speech.
species-lore-diona-roleplay-chaos = “Chaotic Sounds”: an unstable gestalt where nymphs may seize initiative. Speech and actions are inconsistent, but should not disrupt other players’ game.
species-lore-diona-roleplay-ancient = “Ancient Song”: experienced nymphs lead younger ones and direct the gestalt. This character may more often speak as “I.”
species-lore-diona-roleplay-survival = Seek a peaceful and safe solution first. Gestalt survival usually outweighs risk, but a Diona can defend itself and colleagues when violence is unavoidable.
species-lore-vox-lore = Vox are avian sapients with their own culture, language, and independent communities. Many identify with a flock or an arkship, while their speech can sound to other species like a mixture of chirping, cries, and scraping.
species-lore-vox-feature-1 = Their biology is adapted to nitrogen breathing.
species-lore-vox-communication-1 = Vox-pidgin is difficult for other species to reproduce; do not shorten another Vox's name without permission or turn their accent into meaningless noise.
species-lore-vox-communication-2 = Vox-pidgin is natural between Vox. Common can feel coarse and limited; “family,” “home,” and familiar Earth times of day need not mean the same to them.
species-lore-vox-interaction-1 = A flock, shared benefit, and agreement may matter more than formal status. This does not prevent a Vox from cooperating and following crew rules.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-1 = Independence, a flock, freedom, and the exact form of one's name can inform a character without making them a thief or hostile to the crew.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-naming = Use a Vox’s true name or an approximate Common translation. Shortening or corrupting it without consent can be insulting.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-code = The Unbreakable Code: kill less, spend less, protect arkships, protect materials, adapt and evolve. A Vox can break it, but that should be a deliberate part of their story.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-space = Vox are accustomed to hardsuits and zero gravity. EVA work, manoeuvring without gravity, and care for equipment are natural roleplay details.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-solitude = Maintenance tunnels, lockers, and empty spaces can feel safe. Their small stature can also be played in environments built for humans.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-nitrogen = Nitrogen is vital: watch the tank and its gauge. Masks and alien physiology may be uncomfortable, but safety should not be ignored.
species-lore-vox-roleplay-work = For the flock, a Vox may work to exhaustion, but fatigue and the need to recover after a hard shift are also appropriate. Flock interests can conflict with personal wishes.
species-lore-moth-lore = Moths are moth-like humanoids known for wings, soft features, and an interest in light and fabric. Their society survived a severe catastrophe, leaving knowledge, craft, and a safe home especially meaningful.
species-lore-moth-feature-1 = Wings are a key part of their silhouette and appearance.
species-lore-moth-communication-1 = A Moth name often combines an occupation, birthplace, ship, or memorable celestial feature: “Gatherer of Cetus Nebula”, “Keeper of Lyra Spiral”, or “Cartographer of the Dust Ring”.
species-lore-moth-interaction-1 = Wings are painful and vulnerable, so do not touch them without explicit permission. Many Moths are comfortable with friendly touch, but the character's boundaries still matter.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-1 = Curiosity and an appreciation of comfort, clothing, light, or engineering are good hooks, not an entire personality.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-naming = Avoid ordinary human names and overly poetic Diona-like names. A long name should sound like a role or remembered landmark, not random words.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-cleanliness = Care for body, clothing, and a tidy decorated workspace can express respect and goodwill.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-cocoon = Spin a cocoon in private: a Moth is especially vulnerable then. Do not use this custom to obstruct public spaces.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-wings = Wings and antennae can complement emotions: raised antennae show interest, lowered ones anxiety, pressed wings disgust, and a sudden spread anger.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-trust = Friendliness, building trust, and exchanging useful favours are fitting, but should not make the character a manipulator without cause.
species-lore-moth-roleplay-gestures = Combine gestures for complex emotion: asymmetry shows uncertainty, shaking shows intensity, and a sharp posture change shows mixed feelings.
species-lore-arachnid-lore = Arachnids are spider-like sapients with unusual anatomy and varied personal appearances. Their early settlements were often underground and combined ordinary materials with webbing; an unfamiliar form does not make them aggressive.
species-lore-arachnid-feature-1 = Their eyes and additional limbs set them apart from other humanoids.
species-lore-arachnid-roleplay-1 = Account for others' reactions to an unusual appearance without treating the character as inherently hostile. A taste for order, construction, or delicate work is a useful but optional touch.
species-lore-vulpkanin-lore = Vulpkanins are fox-like humanoids with a developed social culture and expressive features. Their stories often centre on honour, personal loyalty, and a path from martial traditions to life in a diverse galaxy.
species-lore-vulpkanin-feature-1 = Ears and tail provide expressive non-verbal cues.
species-lore-vulpkanin-communication-1 = Kanilunz is the main Vulpkanin language. Its sound can be reflected in soft compound names with Finnic, Scandinavian, or Germanic influences.
species-lore-vulpkanin-interaction-1 = Ear and tail reactions can be used as non-verbal cues. Do not assign every Vulpkanin the same culture or attitude toward other species.
species-lore-vulpkanin-roleplay-1 = Use ear and tail movement to complement dialogue. A peaceful, martial, or mixed background is possible, but should not replace the character's own personality.
species-lore-vulpkanin-roleplay-naming = Names may have two to four parts. Examples: “Saivis Lern”, “Mairu’Sen Tuavi”, “Vaeri Nor Sallen”, “Suori’Kei von Riisa”. Avoid human names and joke dog surnames.
species-lore-vulpkanin-roleplay-diet = Lore favours hearty protein-rich food: meat, eggs, and dairy, with plants as a supplement.
species-lore-tajaran-lore = Tajara are feline humanoids with a developed culture, language, and social traditions. Origin, house, or community remain important to many, while others have chosen to live apart from old ties.
species-lore-tajaran-feature-1 = Tail, ears, and digitigrade legs define their silhouette.
species-lore-tajaran-communication-1 = When speaking Galactic Common, a Tajaran usually refers to themself in the third person, uses short phrases, and may draw out the sound “r.”
species-lore-tajaran-communication-2 = This manner is not required when speaking the native language with other Tajaran; individual characters may learn first-person speech.
species-lore-tajaran-interaction-1 = Do not touch a stranger's tail or force hugs: many regard them as personal gestures. Origin, house, or community can shape manners but do not make a character xenophobic.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-1 = Give the character a background and cultural outlook: connection to a house or community, or a life away from them. Do not reduce them to animal mannerisms.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-naming = Follow the naming scheme: a given name, house as a surname, and a house branch where appropriate. For example: “Tek’eraya Kaitam.”
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-colors = Match fur to the chosen subspecies: natural shades are more convincing than random neon colours.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-culture = Choose a house, subspecies, community, or the path of an exile and reflect it in the character’s manners. It is direction, not a requirement to copy someone else’s personality.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-etiquette = Politeness and cleanliness are typical of many Tajaran. Rudeness and profanity should be a deliberate trait of a particular character.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-you = Tajaran commonly use the informal second person: the distinction between singular and formal “you” may seem like an oddity of Galactic Common.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-footwear = Footwraps or suitable footwear are more comfortable for their paws. If ordinary boots are required, discomfort can be roleplayed.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-cleanliness = After dirty work, a Tajaran may seek to clean themself, their clothing, and the workspace.
species-lore-tajaran-roleplay-diet = Meat is prominent in their culture’s diet, though grains and other plant foods are included too. A fondness for dried meat or sliced frozen meat is a fitting biography detail.
species-lore-oni-lore = Oni are tall humanoids whose appearance resembles demons from Earth mythology.
species-lore-oni-feature-1 = Their height and horns make them immediately noticeable.
species-lore-oni-roleplay-1 = Oni may link dignity not to loudness but to reliability: preparation, composure, and seeing a task through.
species-lore-oni-roleplay-order = Show discipline through orderly belongings, checking equipment before work, and quiet confidence. This does not make every Oni a harsh soldier.
species-lore-oni-roleplay-cleanliness = Keeping body, room, and workplace clean can be an expression of respect for self and others, not merely hygiene.
species-lore-felinid-lore = Felinids are feline humanoids with expressive ears and tails.
species-lore-felinid-feature-1 = Ears and tail add non-verbal expressiveness.
species-lore-felinid-roleplay-1 = Feline habits can flavour a character but should not override their profession or personality.
species-lore-harpy-lore = Harpies are avian humanoids with wings and a distinctive appearance.
species-lore-harpy-feature-1 = Wings are a central part of their silhouette.
species-lore-harpy-roleplay-1 = Use curiosity and a fondness for height sparingly and keep cooperation with the crew in mind.
species-lore-resomi-lore = Resomi are small raptor-like sapients, identifiable by their feathers, two pairs of ears, and light build. Their ancestors adapted to a cold world, and names usually have no surname while sometimes preserving pieces of parental names.
species-lore-resomi-feature-1 = Their stature and plumage distinguish them from other humanoids.
species-lore-resomi-communication-1 = Resomi usually use a personal name only, without surname or patronymic. It may occasionally inherit the beginning of a father’s name, the ending of a mother’s name, or both fragments.
species-lore-resomi-communication-2 = When names match, head-feather colour is used as clarification: for example, “Laeri purple.” Neutral name examples: Laeri, Tossi, Verak, Niru.
species-lore-resomi-communication-3 = Resomi speak Shechi, a song-like language of chirping sounds. Suitable syllables include “ka”, “ra”, “ma”, “sha”, “si”, “li”, and “shi”: for example, “Sharimi”, “Kaseli”, “Rinashi”.
species-lore-resomi-interaction-1 = Other Resomi are often treated with familiarity and indulgence, with little regard for the other person’s rank.
species-lore-resomi-interaction-2 = Cultural prejudice toward some other species, especially Vox, may persist. It does not cancel workplace agreements or justify insulting players.
species-lore-resomi-roleplay-1 = Mobility, observation, the value of a name, or cultural habits can enrich an individual character.
species-lore-resomi-roleplay-names = Use one personal name. Inheriting a fragment of a parent’s name is a rare family tradition, not a required rule.
species-lore-resomi-roleplay-orders = Resomi are typically conscientious and work well with direct tasks. An overly vague order can be taken literally or misunderstood.
species-lore-resomi-roleplay-conflict = Force is more often regarded as a response to aggression against oneself, colleagues, or other Resomi, not the first way to settle a dispute.
species-lore-resomi-roleplay-xenophobia = Species prejudice does not require the character to hate anyone: choose a personal stance and do not carry it outside the roleplay.
species-lore-goblin-lore = Goblins are short humanoids known for practicality, invention, and resourcefulness.
species-lore-goblin-feature-1 = Their small stature and expressive appearance are distinctive.
species-lore-goblin-roleplay-1 = Interest in tools and improvisation works well without making greed mandatory.
species-lore-sheleg-lore = Sheleg are tall humanoids with unusual features and a long lifespan.
species-lore-sheleg-feature-1 = Their height and distinct features make them easily recognisable.
species-lore-sheleg-roleplay-1 = A long life may result in patience, experience, or an unusual view of short-term problems.
species-lore-feroxi-lore = Feroxi, also known across the Galaxy as sharks, are shark-like humanoids from Haestar in the Balgaesag-Haest system. Their home belongs to a semi-closed Unified State with its capital at Saegar; their culture grew in vast underwater cities. They are often considered stern and direct, but that bluntness commonly reflects responsibility, respect for agreements, and a willingness to protect those they call their own.
species-lore-feroxi-feature-1 = They move faster in water and do not slip on water surfaces.
species-lore-feroxi-feature-2 = Tail, fins, and body features are important parts of their appearance.
species-lore-feroxi-feature-anatomy = Anatomy: a vascular network helps the body adapt to ambient temperature; in modern culture it is more an inheritance of underwater evolution than a daily necessity. Thick, coarse hair grows only on the head, and the mouth normally has two rows of sharp teeth — around 82–86. The tongue is fleshy with a pointed tip, roughly 17–18 cm long. Pregnancy lasts about ten human months. In-game, this temperature adaptation provides no separate temperature protection.
species-lore-feroxi-interaction-1 = Water, travel, and fleet work can be part of a Feroxi biography, but are not a reason to look down on land-dwelling colleagues.
species-lore-feroxi-roleplay-1 = Account for their aquatic nature, independence, and taste for travel while giving the character goals beyond one biological trait.
species-lore-feroxi-roleplay-family = A large family and long-term commitments matter to many Feroxi. Marriage, children, and loyalty can be important biography details, but are not mandatory for every character.
species-lore-feroxi-roleplay-culture = Directness, principle, persistence, and respect for strength are useful cultural touches. A Feroxi can be martial while still choosing diplomacy, cooperation, and respect for other customs.
species-lore-shadowkin-lore = Shadowkin, also called Tenekin or Twilightkin, are short amphibian-like humanoids from a humid oceanic world. Their culture values sociability, useful work, and the meaning carried by a name.
species-lore-shadowkin-feature-1 = They have an empathic channel: +э text.
species-lore-shadowkin-feature-2 = Their ultraviolet vision is tinted by eye colour; they also have a short shade step.
species-lore-shadowkin-communication-1 = Send to the shared channel with “+э text.” It connects Shadowkin, but is not mind-reading or access to someone else's knowledge.
species-lore-shadowkin-interaction-1 = Sociability and useful contributions to the group are valued. Respect privacy: the shared channel is not permission to disclose someone else's private information.
species-lore-shadowkin-roleplay-1 = The shared channel is a language, not mind-reading; respect privacy and do not use it for out-of-character information. Names may use melodic parts, hyphens, or a number as a family custom.
species-lore-demon-lore = Arcanas are demon-like humanoids with unusual features and individual appearances.
species-lore-demon-feature-1 = Horns, tail, and markings enable a striking silhouette.
species-lore-demon-roleplay-1 = Appearance does not determine morality: choose a personal history and temperament.

# Radiant Sector: structured species reference for the character editor. Lore is separate from game mechanics.
species-lore-human-biology = Human anatomy and needs are the baseline used to compare other species.
species-lore-human-diet = Omnivorous: lore diets commonly include both plant and animal food.
species-lore-human-culture = Human cultures are diverse; origin, accent, and personal history matter more than a single “typical” image.

species-lore-reptilian-biology = Large scaled humanoids with tails, claws, and expressive posture, jaws, and tail movement.
species-lore-reptilian-diet = Meat is prominent in Unathi culture; dishes and habits depend on clan and personal history.
species-lore-reptilian-culture = Honour, independence, directness, and keeping agreements often matter, but clan attitudes to tradition, gender roles, and faith differ.

species-lore-slime-biology = A translucent gelatinous body differs greatly from human biology; water, cold, and the environment require care.
species-lore-slime-diet = They use ordinary food, drink, and medical reagents in-game; lore taste and texture can be perceived differently.
species-lore-slime-culture = Society and habits vary widely; colour, flowing movement, and body shape can express emotion.

species-lore-dwarf-biology = Short, sturdy humanoids; their stature affects life in spaces built for humans.
species-lore-dwarf-diet = Lore favours simple filling food and drinks, with no separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-dwarf-culture = Craft, team reliability, and respect for quality work are frequent cultural themes, not mandatory traits. Khazdar is usually short and clipped, useful for terms of craft, stone, and tools.

species-lore-diona-biology = One adult Diona is a gestalt made of many nymphs. Individual nymphs may have distinct duties and perceptions of time.
species-lore-diona-diet = Plant biology affects taste and habits; do not infer game mechanics from lore food habits unless listed above.
species-lore-diona-culture = Young gestalts often respect elders. Curiosity and peaceful solutions fit the species, while survival of the gestalt remains the highest priority.

species-lore-vox-biology = Small avian humanoids accustomed to hardsuits, zero gravity, and constant monitoring of nitrogen reserves.
species-lore-vox-diet = Vox are lore-wise unselective about food; syntanol and fuel may be familiar substances, not advice on using reagents in-game.
species-lore-vox-culture = Flock and the Unbreakable Code matter more than rank: kill and spend less, protect arks and materials, adapt to circumstances. Breaking the Code can be an intentional story choice.

species-lore-moth-biology = Wings and antennae are sensitive; their position can convey interest, anxiety, disgust, or irritation.
species-lore-moth-diet = Moths use ordinary game food; in lore, food, fabric, and cosy rituals can be part of everyday life.
species-lore-moth-culture = Many Moths value trust, decorated workspaces, and cleanliness. Cocoons are spun privately because a Moth is especially vulnerable then.

species-lore-arachnid-biology = Multi-eyed arachnid anatomy and additional limbs are noticeable at a glance; they do not mean innate aggression.
species-lore-arachnid-diet = They use ordinary game food unless another mechanic says otherwise.
species-lore-arachnid-culture = Underground origins, construction, and web work can enrich a character without requiring them to be reclusive or frightening.

species-lore-vulpkanin-biology = Ears and tail are important non-verbal signals. Their body handles hearty food better than an excess of plant fibre.
species-lore-vulpkanin-diet = Lore diet focuses on meat, eggs, and dairy; plants are a supplement. Alcohol is processed faster, so intoxication may arrive sooner.
species-lore-vulpkanin-culture = Canilunz draws on Finnic, Scandinavian, and Germanic motifs. Names may have two to four parts; human names and joke dog surnames do not fit.

species-lore-tajaran-biology = Predominantly carnivorous mammals with dense fur, claws, balancing tails, and a look strongly shaped by subspecies.
species-lore-tajaran-diet = Meat and grain are prominent in culture. Dried meat and shaved frozen meat fit a biography; food does not change mechanics unless listed above.
species-lore-tajaran-culture = Adomai culture remembers houses, subspecies, and life after liberation. A house, branch, or exile background can guide a character without replacing their personality.

species-lore-oni-biology = Their large build, horns, and physical presence make Oni easy to recognise.
species-lore-oni-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-oni-culture = Discipline is shown through preparation, orderly possessions, composure, and responsibility for results rather than aggression. Kansei is a bridge language made from Japanese speech and the Oni tongue so both sides can learn it without losing familiar phrasing.

species-lore-felinid-biology = Feline ears and tail allow wordless expression; do not touch them without the owner's consent.
species-lore-felinid-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-felinid-culture = There is no mandatory cultural template: feline habits are only an addition to profession and personality.

species-lore-harpy-biology = Wings are an important part of appearance and deserve careful treatment; damage and careless contact can be painful.
species-lore-harpy-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-harpy-culture = Curiosity, interest in heights, and expressive communication fit roleplay if they do not disrupt cooperation. Aerian is melodic: Harpies may mimic voices and everyday sounds, but do not need to turn every phrase into an imitation.

species-lore-resomi-biology = Light feathered bodies, two pairs of ears, and ancestors adapted to cold distinguish Resomi from other species.
species-lore-resomi-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-resomi-culture = A name is usually singular: surnames and patronymics are not used. A rare tradition inherits pieces of parental names; head-feather colour clarifies matching names.

species-lore-goblin-biology = Small stature creates everyday issues in human spaces but does not make a Goblin weak or comic by default.
species-lore-goblin-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-goblin-culture = Practicality, careful use of resources, and inventiveness make good story hooks without demanding greed. Krikli grew from brief work commands and suits quick technical arrangements.

species-lore-sheleg-biology = Tall humanoids with long lifespans and distinctive appearance.
species-lore-sheleg-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-sheleg-culture = A long life can bring patience, experience, and an unusual view of short-term problems without excluding emotion or initiative. Shelar is measured, with extended vowels and pauses that reflect a habit of not rushing conclusions.

species-lore-feroxi-biology = Shark-like humanoids with tails, fins, head-only hair, and two rows of sharp teeth. A vascular network helps adapt to ambient temperature but provides no separate game temperature protection.
species-lore-feroxi-diet = Hearty protein-rich food fits the lore; specific game diet mechanics do not change.
species-lore-feroxi-culture = Their culture grew in underwater cities. Family duties, loyalty to agreements, directness, and respect for strength matter to many Feroxi, but are not mandatory for every individual.

species-lore-shadowkin-biology = Short amphibious humanoids with eyes sensitive to ultraviolet light. Their UV vision uses the selected eye colour.
species-lore-shadowkin-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-shadowkin-culture = Sociability and useful contribution matter in culture. The mental channel connects the species but does not remove privacy or replace normal speech.

species-lore-demon-biology = Horns, tails, and markings give Arcanas varied looks; physiology grants no inherent “demonic” morality.
species-lore-demon-diet = No separate mechanical diet.
species-lore-demon-culture = Arcanas have no single mandatory culture; history, temperament, and relationships are personal choices. Arcanian is melodic and uses many ceremonial forms of address; this is speech style, not proof of mystical power.

# Radiant Sector: actual racial actions and passive traits shown independently from damage modifiers.
species-lore-human-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability: humans have no extreme innate biological restrictions.
species-lore-reptilian-ability-1 = Claws: an unarmed Unathi deals slash damage. This is always active, not a separate button.
species-lore-slime-ability-1 = Passive regeneration: while alive and not too heavily injured, a Slime Person gradually recovers health.
species-lore-dwarf-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability.
species-lore-diona-ability-1 = “Gib Yourself”: after death a gestalt can split into three nymphs; the brain nymph retains the mind and can reform the body after a delay.
species-lore-diona-ability-2 = Kudzu resistance and protection from some floor effects are passive properties of plant biology.
species-lore-vox-ability-1 = Passive toxin damage recovery at low total damage; it has no separate button.
species-lore-moth-ability-1 = They gain speed more quickly in zero gravity. This is a passive movement trait.
species-lore-arachnid-ability-1 = Sericulture: an Arachnid regularly produces silk at the cost of hunger and can use it for crafting.
species-lore-arachnid-ability-2 = They do not get caught in their own webs; this is passive.
species-lore-vulpkanin-ability-1 = “Feral”: an activatable 10-second burst giving +12% movement speed and bonus melee damage. It has a cooldown.
species-lore-tajaran-ability-1 = Claws: a permanent unarmed attack that combines slash and piercing damage.
species-lore-oni-ability-1 = No separate button: extra physical resilience is a passive property of the body.
species-lore-felinid-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability; ears and tail affect appearance and roleplay.
species-lore-harpy-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability; wings are appearance, not guaranteed flight.
species-lore-resomi-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability; cold resistance is passive biology.
species-lore-goblin-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability; resistances and weaknesses are passive.
species-lore-sheleg-ability-1 = No separate active racial ability; cold immunity and other modifiers are passive.
species-lore-feroxi-ability-1 = Water affinity: in water and on water surfaces Feroxi move 15% faster and do not slip.
species-lore-shadowkin-ability-1 = “Shade Step”: an active short movement ability. It spends energy; using it without enough energy causes cellular genetic damage.
species-lore-shadowkin-ability-2 = Ultraviolet vision: a permanent ability tinted by the chosen eye colour.
species-lore-shadowkin-ability-3 = Empathic link: a Shadowkin-only channel using “+э text”, independent of radios and distance.
species-lore-demon-ability-1 = “Arcana aura”: a toggleable ability. When on, every 18 seconds entities within 10 tiles receive an atmospheric text message; it causes no damage or combat effect.
